# Fleet SSH trust — PUBLIC keys only, one per host. Safe to commit.
#
# THIS FILE IS AUTHORITATIVE ON EVERY PLATFORM. Windows (provision/windows.ps1
# → administrators_authorized_keys) regenerates wholesale; POSIX
# (tier_ssh_trust and ssh-wsl.sh step 4 → ~/.ssh/authorized_keys) rewrites a
# managed span
#     # >>> fleet-trust (managed by machines) >>>  …  # <<< fleet-trust <<<
# and leaves every line outside it untouched. A fleet key already present
# outside the span is absorbed into it on the next run. So adding, removing OR
# RENAMING a line here reaches every member.
#
# ⚠ PROPAGATION IS NOT INSTANT — it lands when a box next provisions. A
# revocation you need to take effect NOW must still be applied by hand on each
# member. (It used to never propagate at all on POSIX: the merge was
# append-only and pruned nothing, so a deleted line was revoked on Windows and
# nowhere else. Fixed 2026-08-01.)
#
# ⚠ A REINSTALLED HOST GETS A NEW KEY AND NOTHING ADDS IT HERE AUTOMATICALLY.
# That is exactly how latitude locked itself out of desktop on 2026-08-01: the
# box was rebuilt as Debian, minted a new me@latitude, and this file still
# listed only its dead NixOS-era key. Update this file in the same change that
# rebuilds a host.
#
# Comments are `<login>@<logical fleet name>` — the fleet key / ssh alias /
# tailnet node — NEVER the OS hostname. Hostnames churn and the comments go
# stale: desktop has been DESKTOP-4PQ6V6B, then ME-G614JV, now g614jv; server
# was methe-server until 2026-07-20; latitude still answers `uname -n` with
# latitude5520, a machine that no longer exists. No logical name has ever
# changed. sshd ignores the comment — every human pruning this file does not.
